//? Title: Optimization: Choose the Information Before the Algorithm
//? Subtitle: Full gradients, limited-memory curvature, sampled rows and bounded search
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Minimising a scalar is a common interface, but the information supplied to a
solver determines what its updates and diagnostics mean. We derive first-order,
momentum and limited-memory steps, check a scalar example, and compare native
solvers on known bowls, Rosenbrock, Iris least squares and bounded Rastrigin.
The report introduces the shared API without presenting unequal callback budgets
or finite stochastic runs as a universal method ranking.
)
\toc

> The problem

We seek a coefficient vector \(x\) that makes \(f(x)\) small. A differentiable objective
can supply an exact gradient; a sum of row losses can supply cheap component
gradients; a black-box objective may supply only values. Bounds restrict the
feasible set and alter the relevant optimum. An objective value alone cannot
show stationarity, feasibility, uniqueness or global optimality.

> The method

For smooth unconstrained \(f\), gradient descent uses \(x_{t+1}=x_t-\eta g_t\). With the native
momentum option it instead stores v_next=momentum*v+eta*g and subtracts v_next.
This is a velocity evaluated at x, rather than the look-ahead gradient used by
the separate Nesterov solver. Every optimize call resets its velocity.
For a scalar quadratic \(f(x)=\tfrac12 hx^2\), zero momentum gives

$$
 x_t=(1-\eta h)^t x_0,\qquad
 f(x_t)=\tfrac12 h(1-\eta h)^{2t}x_0^2.
$$
\alttext(On a positive-curvature scalar quadratic, gradient descent multiplies the coordinate by one minus eta times curvature at every step. The objective contracts by the square of that factor.)

Convergence requires 0<eta*h<two. Small eta is slow, and the boundary eta*h=two
flips the sign with unchanged loss. Native gradient descent tests the gradient it just used or absolute loss
change, so it can return at that boundary with a large gradient. Normal return
must therefore be followed by a diagnostic suited to the problem.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_overview_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_overview_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_overview_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_overview_mechanism.svg build/opt_overview_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_overview_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_overview_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Choose a solver from the available information, then verify its returned point.)
\alttext(An objective interface determines the available information. Explicit solver controls produce a point and a callback history, which require problem-specific checks.)

Adam stores exponential first/second moments with bias correction, then divides
its first moment by sqrt(corrected second moment)+epsilon. In this API it uses
the full supplied gradient; it does not select stochastic rows automatically.
L-BFGS approximates inverse curvature using recent displacement/gradient pairs,
with O(history_size*p) storage and a weak-Wolfe line search. Positive-curvature
pairs are retained; a failed finite search can use a previous Armijo step or
return without a new step. It is a local method, not a global optimizer.

A full dense gradient costs O(n*p) for least squares. Mean mini-batch SGD costs
O(batch_size*p) per update but can retain row noise. Value-only population methods
spend many function evaluations exploring a box. Callback counts expose these
requests, not their wall-clock cost. The linked focused reports derive
[adaptive memories](optimization_adaptive_gradient_analysis.mepml),
[coordinate and conjugate-gradient steps](optimization_coordinate_cg_analysis.mepml),
[curvature methods](optimization_second_order_analysis.mepml),
[constraints](optimization_constrained_analysis.mepml),
[proximal maps](optimization_proximal_analysis.mepml),
[residual least squares](optimization_nonlinear_least_squares_analysis.mepml),
[derivative-free search](optimization_derivative_free_analysis.mepml) and
[variance reduction](optimization_variance_reduction_analysis.mepml).

> Small worked example

For \(f(x)=x^2\), start two, \(\eta=.1\) and momentum .5, the first gradient is four,
the first velocity .4 and the first point 1.6. The next gradient is 3.2,
velocity .52 and point 1.08. Zero-momentum gradient descent instead reaches 1.28
after two steps. Adam with the same eta, default moment weights and epsilon
makes its first point approximately 1.9 because its bias corrections cancel
on the first gradient. Equal numeric rates do not mean equal displacements.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_overview_first_steps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective     f({2}, {0});
  o::GradientDescentOptions c;
  c.step_size      = .1;
  c.max_iterations = 2;
  c.tolerance      = 0;
  auto a           = o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::GradientDescent(c), {2});
  c.momentum       = .5;
  auto           b = o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::GradientDescent(c), {2});
  o::AdamOptions d;
  d.step_size      = .1;
  d.max_iterations = 2;
  d.tolerance      = 0;
  auto e           = o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::Adam(d), {2});
  o::optimization_comparison(f, {a, b, e}, {"gradient descent", "momentum .5", "Adam"})
      .title("Two updates: rates have method-specific meanings")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_overview_first_steps.svg");
  for (const auto& run : {a, b, e})
    std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\n", run.trace.values().to_md(8), run.summary().to_md(8));
}
```
