> Checking the theory

>> A known bowl and its gradient certificate

Use f=sum_j(x_j-centre_j) squared, centre (4,-2,1), and start (10,10,10).
GD uses eta=.1, momentum GD eta=.1 with .8 momentum, Adam eta=.1, each with
2000 updates and tolerance 1e-10; L-BFGS uses 100 updates and tolerance 1e-10.
The analytic minimizer and zero objective give an exact reference. All methods
receive the same full-gradient interface, although L-BFGS line-search trials
change its evaluation cost. The experiment checks these configured runs rather
than ranking methods after equal wall time or extensive tuning.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_overview_bowl.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective     f({2, 2, 2}, {4, -2, 1});
  o::GradientDescentOptions g;
  g.step_size      = .1;
  g.max_iterations = 2000;
  g.tolerance      = 1e-10;
  auto m           = g;
  m.momentum       = .8;
  o::AdamOptions a;
  a.step_size      = .1;
  a.max_iterations = 2000;
  a.tolerance      = 1e-10;
  o::LBFGSOptions l;
  l.max_iterations = 100;
  l.tolerance      = 1e-10;
  const std::vector<double>       start{10, 10, 10};
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs{
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::GradientDescent(g), start),
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::GradientDescent(m), start),
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::Adam(a), start),
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::LBFGS(l), start)};
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"GD", "momentum GD", "Adam", "L-BFGS"};
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, names)
      .title("Known bowl: check the returned gradient")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_overview_bowl.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print(
        "{}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\n",
        names[i],
        runs[i].summary().to_md(7),
        runs[i].trace.counts().to_md(0));
}
```
