>> A curved valley changes the numerical problem

Rosenbrock is f=(1-x) squared+100*(y-x squared) squared, with unique global
minimum (1,1) and zero loss. From (-1.2,1), GD uses eta=.001 and momentum zero;
Adam uses eta=.01. Both allow 20000 updates with tolerance 1e-10. L-BFGS uses
1000 updates, history ten and tolerance 1e-10. Rosenbrock's narrow, curved valley
adds changing coupled curvature; success on a spherical bowl does not predict
progress here. The value-request curve includes rejected line-search trials.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_overview_rosenbrock.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::SmoothBenchmark        f;
  o::GradientDescentOptions g;
  g.step_size      = .001;
  g.max_iterations = 20000;
  g.tolerance      = 1e-10;
  o::AdamOptions a;
  a.step_size      = .01;
  a.max_iterations = 20000;
  a.tolerance      = 1e-10;
  o::LBFGSOptions l;
  l.max_iterations = 1000;
  l.tolerance      = 1e-10;
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs{
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::GradientDescent(g), {-1.2, 1}),
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::Adam(a), {-1.2, 1}),
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::LBFGS(l), {-1.2, 1})};
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"GD .001", "Adam .01", "L-BFGS"};
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, names)
      .title("Rosenbrock: a local curved valley")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_overview_rosenbrock.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\n", names[i], runs[i].summary().to_md(8));
}
```
