> Iris: connect sampled updates to an algebraic solution

Predict Petal.Length from Sepal.Length, Sepal.Width and Petal.Width, with an
intercept and all 150 observed flowers. The numeric Formula design uses raw
centimetres. The direct linear-model fit solves the same unpenalised least-squares
problem, providing the full-sum minimum RSS/2. SGD averages batch gradients,
while f remains the sum of .5*residual squared. Use rates .001 and .01,
batch size ten, 500 epochs, seed 42, shuffling enabled and tolerance zero.
The loss is a training numerical control; the table of Species composition is
context, and no held-out predictive performance is inferred.

Ordinary least squares has nonzero residuals here, so constant-rate sampling
can keep fluctuating near the solution. A small loss gap can coexist with
coefficient error in small-curvature directions. Compare the explicit final
coefficient difference and mean full-gradient norm, not only the plotted loss.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_overview_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o                 = dm::optim;
  const auto          iris    = dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::string   formula = "Petal.Length ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Width";
  auto                f       = o::LeastSquaresObjective::from_data(iris, formula);
  const dm::stats::LM ref(iris, formula);
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  std::vector<std::string>        names;
  for (double eta : {.001, .01}) {
    o::SGDOptions s;
    s.step_size  = eta;
    s.batch_size = 10;
    s.max_epochs = 500;
    s.tolerance  = 0;
    runs.push_back(
        o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableSeparableFunction>(
            f, o::SGD(s), std::vector<double>(4, 0)));
    names.push_back(std::format("SGD {}", eta));
    double error = 0;
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 4; ++j)
      error = std::max(error, std::abs(runs.back().coordinates[j] - ref.coefficients()[j]));
    std::print(
        "{}: max coefficient difference={:.8g}, mean gradient norm={:.8g}.\n\n{}\n\n",
        names.back(),
        error,
        runs.back().gradient_norm / 150,
        runs.back().summary().to_md(8));
  }
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, names, "value", ref.rss() / 2, false, true)
      .title("Iris: SGD versus the direct least-squares reference")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_overview_iris.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\nDirect full-sum minimum={:.10g}.\n",
      (iris | dm::group_by("Species") |
       dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"), dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length", "mean_length_cm")))
          .to_md(4),
      ref.rss() / 2);
}
```
